.docname {Notification Schema}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Crate | A Rust library other Rust code builds against | `notification-schema` |
    | Notification | One message to people of one domain | "Lab 2 Temperature is 31.4 °C" |
    | Bell | The in-app list of messages in the browser | Top bar icon |
    | Web Push | A pop-up the browser shows even with the page closed | Sent through the browser maker's push server |
    | Service worker | A browser script that receives Web Push | `frontend/public/service-worker.js` |
    | Severity | How serious a message is: `info`, `warning`, `danger` | `danger` for a breach |
    | Domain | A group of people and buildings inside an organization | `eng` |
    | Room (socket) | A named group of browser connections | `domain:eng` |
    | Fixture | A JSON file of example messages every language must agree on | `schemas/fixtures/notification.json` |

Rust crate at `schemas/notification-schema`. It holds the one message `notification` sends to people, and the Redis channel it travels on.

Depends only on `serde` and `serde_json`. Tests also use `jsonschema`.

---

## Why one shape for two deliveries

`notification` delivers every message twice:

- to the **bell**, through Redis and `socket`;
- as a **Web Push** pop-up, through the browser's push server.

Both get the **same** `Notification` value, serialised by the same code. So the bell and the pop-up can never show different text for one event.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        N["notification<br/>builds Notification"] -- "Redis channel notifications" --> S["socket<br/>routes on domainName"]
        S -- "event notification to room domain:eng" --> B["frontend bell<br/>type, message, timestamp"]
        N -- "Web Push, same JSON, encrypted" --> W["service worker<br/>title, message, icon"]

---

## What is inside

| Item | Value | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| `NOTIFICATIONS_CHANNEL` | `notifications` | `notification` publishes, `socket` subscribes |
| `Severity` | `info`, `warning`, `danger` | Field `type`; `FromStr` parses the text of `POST /notification/trigger` |
| `Notification` | The message | `notification` builds, `socket` parses, browser reads |

---

## The message

```json
{"id":"1757251200000","type":"danger",
 "title":"Temperature Alert - Innovation Hub",
 "message":"Innovation Hub : Lab 2 Temperature is 31.4 °C (above maximum)",
 "timestamp":"2025-09-07T13:20:00.000Z","domainName":"eng","metric":"temperature"}
```

| Field | Rust | Rule | Read by |
|---|---|---|---|
| `id` | `id: String` | Millisecond clock at build time, as text | Bell, to tell items apart |
| `type` | `r#type: Severity` | Only `info`, `warning`, `danger`. Anything else fails to parse | Bell colour |
| `title` | `title` | Required | Pop-up heading |
| `message` | `message` | Required | Bell text and pop-up body |
| `timestamp` | `timestamp: String` | ISO-8601 in UTC with milliseconds, never a number | Bell |
| `domainName` | `domain_name` | Required **and** not empty | `socket`, to pick the room |
| `metric` | `Option<String>` | Set on a breach only. Left out when empty | Bell filter |
| `icon` | `Option<String>` | Left out when empty | Service worker |

- `type` is the severity, **not** the metric. The metric sits in `metric`.
- `domainName` has its own check, `non_empty`. A plain `String` would accept `""`, and `""` names no room.

### Why a missing domain must fail

`socket` routes by `domainName` and never broadcasts. If a message with no domain parsed, `socket` would have no room for it. The safe outcome is "not a notification": it is skipped.

**Example.** `{"type":"danger",…,"domainName":""}`

1. `socket` calls `serde_json::from_str::<Notification>`.
2. `non_empty` sees `""` and returns `must not be empty`.
3. `get_notification_delivery_plan` returns `None`. `socket` counts it as skipped. No browser gets it.

---

## A breach notification end to end

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant A as notification<br/>Alerts::on_breach
        participant Dm as domain/notification.rs
        participant R as Redis
        participant S as socket<br/>shell/server.rs
        participant P as notification<br/>Push::to_domain
        participant W as Push server
        participant B as Browser
        A->>A: domains_for_building gives eng
        A->>A: fan_out, one round per domain
        A->>Dm: notification(now, now, Danger, title, message, eng)
        Dm-->>A: Notification, metric set to temperature
        A->>R: RedisBus publish: serde_json::to_string on notifications
        R->>S: message on notifications
        S->>S: consume, then get_notification_delivery_plan(text)
        S->>S: parse Notification, room_for_domain gives domain:eng
        S->>B: emit notification to room domain:eng
        B->>B: isVisible(message, preferences), then show in the bell
        A->>P: to_domain(sent, eng, temperature)
        P->>P: accounts_subscribed_to(eng, temperature)
        P->>W: WebPushSender send: serde_json::to_vec, encrypt, POST
        W->>B: push event
        B->>B: service worker: showNotification(title, body = message)

### Data at each step

Example: a 31.4 °C breach in Lab 2 of Innovation Hub, building owned by domain `eng`, clock at 1757251200000.

| Step | Holder | Data |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `on_breach` | `["eng"]` |
| 3 | arguments | id and time 1757251200000, `Danger`, title `Temperature Alert - Innovation Hub`, message `Innovation Hub : Lab 2 Temperature is 31.4 °C (above maximum)`, domain `eng` |
| 4 | `Notification` | the JSON above; `timestamp` from `iso8601(1757251200000)` is `2025-09-07T13:20:00.000Z` |
| 5 | Text on `notifications` | the same JSON as one string |
| 8 | `Delivery` in `socket` | room `domain:eng`, payload the parsed JSON |
| 10 | Bell | shown only if `preferences["eng"]["temperature"] === true` |
| 12 | Accounts | e.g. `["ada"]`: only accounts that switched `temperature` on for `eng` |
| 13 | Push body | the same JSON as bytes, then encrypted with the subscription's keys |
| 15 | Pop-up | heading `Temperature Alert - Innovation Hub`, body the message, no icon |

### What the bell decides

`frontend/src/utils/commons/notification.ts`, `isVisible`:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        M["notification arrives"] --> Q{"metric present?"}
        Q -- no --> Show["show: manual alert, nothing to check"]
        Q -- yes --> P{"preferences[domainName][metric] is true?"}
        P -- yes --> Show2["show"]
        P -- no --> Hide["hide"]

Push makes the same choice on the server, in `accounts_subscribed_to`. So one rule, applied in two places.

---

## A manual notification

`POST /notification/trigger` builds the same shape, through `Alerts::trigger`:

1. Empty `message`? Use `Manual Alert Triggered`.
2. `type` given? Parse it with `Severity::from_str`. `alert` or any other word gives `400 type must be one of: info, warning, danger`. No `type`: `danger`.
3. `buildingName` missing: `400`.
4. Find the domains of the building, then keep only the ones the caller may reach.
5. One notification per kept domain, title `Ambitwin Alert`, **no `metric`**. So the bell always shows it.

**Example.** `{"message":"Fire drill at 15:00","type":"warning","buildingName":"Innovation Hub"}` from a member of `eng` gives one `warning` to room `domain:eng`.

---

## Tests

| Where | Checks |
|---|---|
| `tests/notification_conformance.rs` | Every fixture case re-serialises **byte for byte**; every rejected case fails; `Severity` parses only its own names; channel is `notifications` |
| `tests/notification_schema.rs` | Every fixture case passes `schemas/json/notification.schema.json`; every rejected case fails it |
| `tests/notification_preferences_schema.rs` | The `GET` / `POST /notification/preferences` fixture against its schema |
| `notification` `domain/notification.rs` | `id` is the clock as text; `timestamp` like `2023-11-14T22:13:20.123Z`; `type` not `kind`; no `icon` by default |
| `socket` `core/relay.rs` | Every case routed to its domain room, every rejection skipped |
| `frontend` `notification.spec.ts` | Every severity has a colour; `isVisible` follows the stored preferences |

Rejected cases in the fixture: no `domainName`, empty `domainName`, `type: "alert"`, no `title`, a number as `timestamp`.

The preferences fixture has **no** Rust type here: only `notification` parses it, and it reads raw JSON. The crate hosts the schema check because it already depends on `jsonschema`.

```bash
mise exec -- cargo test --manifest-path schemas/notification-schema/Cargo.toml
```
